WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.772
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.358 --> 00:00:06.150
SPEAKER: Welcome to
the quickstart tutorial

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.300
for BigQuery, Google Cloud's
large-scale data warehouse

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.023
for storage and analysis.

00:00:12.023 --> 00:00:13.440
In this video,
we're going to walk

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.320
through how to analyze data
stored in BigQuery using the BQ

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.340
command-line tool.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.300
To start, visit
console.cloud.google.com

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.370
to open the Google
Cloud console.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:27.130
This web portal allows
you to manage and explore

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:30.500
your Cloud resources
directly from a browser.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.482
Create a new project by clicking
the Project Viewer button

00:00:33.482 --> 00:00:34.565
at the top of your screen.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.250
Once it's ready, you'll get
a notification saying so.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.650
Click the Notifications
icon, and then

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.070
click Select Project to
open your new project.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.160
Next, activate Cloud
Shell by clicking its icon

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.870
to the right of the search bar.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.420
Cloud Shell is an online
development environment

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.490
that comes preloaded
with helpful tools

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.190
for interacting with
your Cloud resources.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.129
Once it launches, click
the Maximize button

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:07.440
to increase the size of
the Cloud Shell terminal.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.570
Now you'll use the
BQ command-line tool

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.190
to explore one of
the sample tables

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.040
provided in BigQuery, the
Shakespeare table, which

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.730
contains an entry for every
word in every Shakespeare play.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:21.180
You can use the bq show
command to display information

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.500
about the table.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.590
Type bq show plus the table
reference and the command

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.370
prompt.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.610
If prompted, make sure to
click Authorize to authorize

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.118
Cloud Shell to make API calls.

00:01:33.118 --> 00:01:35.410
You will then see information
about the table returned,

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:38.650
including a schema and size.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:41.410
You can learn more about how
to use the BQ command-line tool

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.060
by typing in bq help.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.150
You can also view information
about a specific command.

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:53.410
Here, we can ask for information
about the bq query command.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:56.350
Now let's run an SQL query
over the Shakespeare table

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:58.630
using the bq query command.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.730
This query, which is
available following

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.410
the link in the
description below,

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.230
calculates how many times
the substring raisin

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:08.039
appears in Shakespeare's works.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:10.770
The output shows that
while raisins appears once,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.080
the raisin substring
also appears in words

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.970
like praising and raising.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.640
We can also query to see
how many times the substring

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.520
huzzah appears.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.430
Since the substring does not
appear in Shakespeare's works,

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.290
no results are returned.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.860
Now let's create a new table
and place it in a new data set.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.530
Our sample data will come
from the US Social Security

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.710
Administration, and contains
about seven megabytes of data

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.060
about popular baby names.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.220
First, visit the link
in the description

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.110
below and download
the baby names

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.810
zip file to your local device.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:48.620
Open the file YOB2010.txt
to see what it looks like.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.700
It contains comma-separated
values for three columns--

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.480
name, sex, and number of
children with that name.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.910
Next, upload the YOB2010.txt
file from your local device

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:04.380
to Cloud Shell.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.450
Using the three-dotted
More menu,

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.580
you can confirm that the
file was successfully

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.330
uploaded to the Cloud Shell
environment by running ls.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:17.370
Run bq ls to check whether your
project has existing data sets.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.270
Now we can run the bq mk command
to create a new data set called

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.670
baby names for our own project.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:27.120
If we run bq ls again, we
can see that our new data set

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.350
appears as part of our project.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:33.800
In the baby names data set that
you created, use the bq load

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.900
command to load
your source file,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:41.650
YOB2010.txt, into a new
table called Names 2010.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.050
This command creates a table
and loads data in a single step.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:49.810
You can provide the data set ID,
table ID, source, and schema.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:56.440
Once the upload is complete,
confirm that the table now

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:01.960
appears in the data set by
running bq ls babynames.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:03.390
You can also view
the table schema

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:09.040
by running bq show,
babynames.names2010.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.810
Now that the table
is loaded, we can

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:14.020
run some queries on the data,
again using the bq query

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:14.920
command.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:19.570
For example, this query returns
the most popular girls' names.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:23.560
And this one returns
some unusual boys' names.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.630
Now that you've successfully
run your queries,

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.810
make sure to complete a
few quick cleanup steps.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.540
To avoid incurring storage
charges to your Google Cloud

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.570
account for the resources
used in this quickstart,

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:38.260
run this bq rm command and
confirm the Delete command

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.290
by typing in y.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:45.030
To finish cleanup,
type rm YOB2010.txt

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.430
to remove the file that you
uploaded to your Cloud Shell

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.060
environment.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.050
And that's it.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.300
You're now equipped to
run queries and load data

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.730
using the BQ command-line tool.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:57.760
Make sure to check out the rest
of our BigQuery documentation

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.990
at
cloud.google.com/BigQuery/Docs

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.020
to learn more about how to use
BigQuery to gain insights about

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:06.040
your data.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:10.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

